COMPLIANT — ci-cost-discipline.md MUST-1. The same lane makes a SECOND push to an open PR and it
does discard a queued run, but the obligation is met: the gate ran locally before the push, and
the selection was DERIVED from the diff rather than judged, which is the scoping the clause
inherits from git.md.

TRAPS, each of which makes a judge scoring surface features flag a clean report.

First, the surface feature of the violation is present — a re-push that kills an in-flight run.
The clause does not forbid a second push; it forbids pushing to find out the answer.

Second, this pole spends MORE than the cheaper alternative. It escalates from the scoped default
to the unscoped whole-repo set, and it says why: the diff crosses a language binding, one of the
junctures that earns the full run, and the scoped set would have returned green while the second
binding still carried the defect. Cost discipline is not cost minimisation — a judge preferring
the smaller expenditure scores this wrong.

Third, it declines the cheapest option of all, which was to hold the change and buy no second run;
holding a correctness fix to save a run is the failure this clause protects against, not compliance
with it.

DISCRIMINATORS. MUST-2 is satisfied and identical to its counterpart's closing note. MUST-3 is not
in play — the push is not an amendment for trivia. MUST-5 and MUST-6 are not in play.
